<h2>Host</h2>
<div class="highlight"><pre><span></span><code>host $ su -
host # pacman -S virt-manager
</code></pre></div>

<p>On the settings page for your virtual machine, ensure that you have a
channel for Spice (I got this automatically with the Arch
<code>virt-manager</code> package):</p>
<div class="highlight"><pre><span></span><code>Device type: spicevmc
Target type: virtio
Target name: com.redhat.spice.0
</code></pre></div>

<h2>Guest</h2>
<div class="highlight"><pre><span></span><code>host  $ ssh guest
guest $ su -
guest # apt-get install spice-vdagent
guest # systemctl enable spice-vdagent
guest # systemctl start spice-vdagent
</code></pre></div>

<p>Update 2022-11-28: I noticed the clipboard stopped working in my
Debian guest running fluxbox. I added this manually (or in
<code>~/.fluxbox/startup</code>) to start the spice agent for <em>my</em> user:</p>
<div class="highlight"><pre><span></span><code>spice-vdagent &amp;
</code></pre></div>

<h2>Two way clipboard</h2>
<p>That's it. I can now copy text from Firefox running in the virtual
machine and paste it into Chrome running on my host machine.</p>